AB  - The Pacific Islands hold a unique position in understanding and addressing climate change. The role of the ocean in moderating climate impacts in the Pacific region is poorly understood, in part because of a lack of observations. Collaboratively establishing a network of marine stations can not only contextualize local environments in a broader regional setting, but also provide unique information that can benefit the marine stations across the Pacific pool resources and be a powerful regional and global leader in understanding the role of the ocean in climate change. It can also play a pivotal role in evaluating the efficacy of marine carbon dioxide removal approaches while also crafting responsive environmental laws that ensure the preservation and responsible use of marine ecosystems, cultural heritage, and indigenous knowledge.
