TI  - Seismic redundancy of steel buildings with brittle connections
T2  - APPLICATIONS OF STATISTICS AND PROBABILITY, VOLS 1 AND 2: CIVIL ENGINEERING RELIABILITY AND RISK ANALYSIS
LA  - English
CP  - 8th International Conference on Applications of Statistics and Probability (ICASP 8)
KW  - RELIABILITY
AB  - The redundancy of building structures under earthquakes has attracted more attention after the 1994 Northridge and 1995 Kobe earthquakes. Very little, however, is known on redundancy under stochastic dynamic loads primarily due to the analytical difficulty associated with such problems. In this paper, a method is proposed for evaluation of structural reliability and redundancy of steel buildings under earthquakes. The seismic hazard is expressed in terms of annual exceedance probability of bi-axial spectral displacement (BSD) and structural performance is measured by maximum column drift ratio (MCDR). The response statistics under the SAC ground motions are obtained and the effects of brittle connection failure, biaxial interaction, and torsional motion are examined. A power-law relation between MCDR and BSD is established from regression analyses of building response under SAC ground motions. The annual probability of exceeding a given MCDR threshold is then determined from the BSD hazard curve multiplied by a correction factor for the capacity and modeling uncertainties. A uniform-risk redundancy factor is also developed for calculating the required design force such that structures of different degrees of redundancy can satisfy a uniform reliability requirement.
