AB  - This study proposes an upcycling strategy to convert commercially degraded porous high-density polyethylene (PE) membranes, originally designed for water treatment, into gas-liquid hollow fiber membrane contactors (GL-HFMCs) for direct ocean capture (DOC) via polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) coating. GL-HFMC employs porous hydrophobic membranes to simultaneously achieve high mass transfer and pore wetting resistance, while a trade-off between porosity and pore size leads to increased fabrication complexity and cost. Commercial PE membranes for water treatment offer scalable and low-cost platforms with inherently porous structures, but have physically incorporated hydrophilic additives and relatively large pore sizes, limiting direct application. During practical operation, fouling-cleaning cycles can induce partial additive loss, resulting in a post-cleaning state where the porous framework is largely preserved while intrinsic PE hydrophobicity is partially recovered. Accordingly, additive-depleted membranes were employed as a controlled substrate representing this condition, and their relevance was verified through comparison with fouling-cleaning-treated membranes. However, the relatively large pore size still promotes liquid entry under GL-HFMC conditions. To address this limitation, PDMS coating was applied to regulate pore structure and enhance hydrophobicity. The optimized PE/20PDMS membrane achieved a CO2 stripping flux of 9.82 & times; 10-6 kg m-2 s-1 (pH 4.5 and 25 degrees C) with 93% flux retention about 100 h operation, with minor decline attributed to partial pore wetting. This work demonstrates that degradation-induced surface changes can be leveraged for cost-effective design of scalable GL-HFMC systems for DOC.
