TI  - Valorization of Na2SO4 in wastewater from spent lithium-ion battery recycling using redox-mediated bipolar membrane electrodialysis
T2  - CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA  - English
KW  - Wastewater
KW  - Sodium sulfate
KW  - pH swing
KW  - Redox-mediated electrodialysis
KW  - Bipolar membrane
KW  - EXCHANGE MEMBRANES
KW  - RECOVERY
KW  - SALT
AB  - The presence of sodium sulfate (Na2SO4) in wastewater poses a significant challenge to lithium-ion battery recycling. Bipolar membrane electrodialysis (BMED) has been explored to address this issue by electrochemically removing Na2SO4 while simultaneously producing sulfuric acid (H2SO4) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) through a bipolar membrane. However, traditional BMED faces drawbacks, such as high energy use, low economic viability, fouling, and complex modularity. This study introduces redox-mediated bipolar membrane electrodialysis (redox-BMED) as a novel alternative. This approach replaces the water-splitting reaction in BMED with a sustainable redox reaction of ferri-/ferrocyanide, enabling operation at a low cell voltage of 1.2 V, enhancing energy efficiency, and simplifying modularity with porous carbon electrode stacking. The redox-BMED system effectively manages Na2SO4 and produces H2SO4 and NaOH with reduced energy consumption (0.55 kWh/kg Na2SO4) and operational expenses (5.30 k$/yr), which are over 50 % lower than those of conventional BMED. Optimization of the membrane arrangement and parametric analysis across various operational conditions (e.g., cell voltage, feed concentration, flow rate, and ion selectivity) further enhanced the system's efficiency. This technology offers a promising solution for environmentally friendly wastewater treatment, resource recovery, and direct ocean capture requiring deionization with a simultaneous pH swing.
